// Rotations: continuous control over a qubit, and angle expressions.
//
// Concept
//   Where X is a half-turn about the X axis of the Bloch sphere, rx(theta) is a
//   turn by any angle. This is how a qubit is tuned to an arbitrary
//   superposition rather than just an equal one.
//
//   `const` lets an angle be named once and reused. Angle expressions are
//   evaluated at compile time, so the IR contains a plain number: run
//   `qforge inspect examples/rotations.qf --ir` to see that.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   ry(theta)|0> = cos(theta/2)|0> + sin(theta/2)|1>.
//   With theta = pi/3: cos(pi/6) = sqrt(3)/2 and sin(pi/6) = 1/2, so the
//   probability of outcome 1 is exactly 1/4.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `result` is 1 in about 25% of shots and 0 in about 75%.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   The half-angle is not a typo: a rotation by 2*pi returns -I, not I, and a
//   qubit needs a full 4*pi to come back to itself. That factor of two is why
//   QForge's optimiser only cancels a rotation at multiples of 4*pi — see
//   docs/compiler-design.md.
//
// @expect qubits=1 clbits=1 gates=1
// @expect-state |0>=0.75
// @expect-state |1>=0.25
// @expect-support 0,1
// @expect-frequency 1=0.25

const theta = pi / 3;

qubit q;
bit result;

ry(theta) q;

measure q -> result;
